    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, ruled by many separate kings rather than one.</li><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> abandoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes</a> around 260 they settled the lands of later Swabia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</li><li>Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> around 496–506, they became the Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-alamannia">Duchy of Alamannia</a>; their dialects survive today.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The French word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, Allemagne, and the Spanish Alemania come from the Alemanni, one people of the south-west, not from the Germans as a whole.</p></aside>
    </section>

