    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cassius-dio.jpg" alt="Cassius Dio" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dio_Cassius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alemanni appear on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the Main in the early 3rd century, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s German frontier came under new pressure. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> campaigned against them in 213, although some scholars argue that the name was added by a later excerptor and that the first certain mention comes in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> panegyric of 289. The Roman writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asinius-quadratus">Asinius Quadratus</a>, quoted by the 6th-century historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathias">Agathias</a>, explained the name as meaning a mixed and motley crowd, and it is usually understood as ‘all men’. The confederation seems to have formed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe-germanic-peoples">Elbe Germanic</a> groups, among them Suebian peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semnones">Semnones</a>, who moved south-west towards the Roman frontier in the late 2nd and early 3rd centuries and merged with the people they found there. In the 230s raids, probably Alemannic, into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agri-decumates">Agri Decumates</a>, the Roman territory between the upper Rhine and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severus-alexander">Severus Alexander</a> to the frontier. During the crisis of the mid-3rd century, around 259–260, Rome abandoned the fortified Upper German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetian-limes">Raetian Limes</a>, and the Alemanni gradually settled the land between the Rhine, the Danube and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, the heartland of later Swabia. Their settlement was a slow occupation of farmland rather than a single conquest, and its pace is still debated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bad-urach"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bad-urach.jpg" alt="Bad Urach" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bad-urach">Bad Urach</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BadUrachMarktplatz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ssch, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alemannic society was rural and organised around kin groups and the households of a warrior aristocracy. Most people lived in small hamlets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> longhouses and sunken-floored huts, often close to abandoned Roman villas whose stone buildings they rarely reused, growing barley, wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">rye</a> and raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and pigs. From the 4th century the elite built fortified hilltop centres, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/runder-berg">Runder Berg</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-urach">Bad Urach</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">Zähringer</a> Burgberg near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg</a>, where archaeologists have found evidence of metalworking and fine jewellery production. Roman coin, glass and luxury goods reached them through trade, raiding and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> paid to friendly kings, and Roman gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> in Alemannic graves shows how closely they were bound to the empire’s economy. Many young men served in the Roman army and returned with pay and prestige. In the later 5th century large row-grave cemeteries appear, with men buried with sword, shield and spear and women with brooches and bead necklaces. The Alemanni were pagan; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathias">Agathias</a> says that they worshipped trees, rivers, hills and ravines and sacrificed horses and cattle to them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread only slowly, after they came under Frankish rule in the 6th and 7th centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gallienus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallienus.jpg" alt="Gallienus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_gallieno_dalla_casa_delle_vestali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sailko, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For almost three centuries the Alemanni were among the most persistent enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Their armies were raiding bands rather than standing forces, made up of the retinues of kings and nobles, mostly foot soldiers with spears, shields and swords, joined by mounted nobles. Around 260 a great raid reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> defeated it near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and in 268 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius II</a> defeated another army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-garda">Lake Garda</a>. Juthungi and Alemanni invaded Italy again in 270–271, defeating Aurelian near Placentia before he destroyed them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fano">Fano</a> and Pavia. In the 350s, during the civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnentius">Magnentius</a>, Alemannic kings overran the Rhine frontier and occupied land on its west bank. Julian restored it, and in 357 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-strasbourg">Battle of Strasbourg</a> his army of about 13,000 defeated a coalition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chnodomar">Chnodomar</a> and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapio">Serapio</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> put at some 35,000 men. Chnodomar was captured and died in Rome. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> fought them again after the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rando">Rando</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in 368, winning a hard battle at Solicinium, and in 378 Gratian defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lentienses">Lentienses</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-argentovaria">Argentaria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. In 457 troops of Majorian destroyed a band of some 900 Alemanni who had crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> into Italy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus.jpg" alt="Ammianus Marcellinus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ammianus_Marcellinus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Author of the original work of art is long dead, while the uploader of the picture portraying it released it under a CC BY 3.0 license., CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alemanni never formed a single kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> describes many kings (<em>reges</em>) and lesser princes, each ruling a district with his own following, and he names subgroups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucinobantes">Bucinobantes</a> opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lentienses">Lentienses</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, the Brisigavi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breisgau">Breisgau</a> and the Juthungi north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Kingship could pass within families: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chnodomar">Chnodomar</a>’s nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapio">Serapio</a> had been given a Greek name by his father, who had learned Greek religious mysteries while a hostage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. In wartime several kings joined under a leading figure, as at Strasbourg, where Ammianus lists Chnodomar and Serapio with five further kings, ten princes and many nobles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> dealt with these rulers individually, through treaties, gifts, hostages and, when needed, force. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadomarius">Vadomarius</a> of the Breisgau was arrested by Julian’s agents in 361 and later served the empire as a general in the East, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> failed to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macrianus-alemannic-king">Macrianus</a> of the Bucinobantes, tried to replace him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraomarius">Fraomarius</a>, and finally made peace with him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> near Mainz in 374. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> speaks of a single Alemannic king killed in battle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>, which may indicate greater unity by the late 5th century. Written law came only under Frankish rule, in the 7th-century <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pactus-alamannorum">Pactus Alamannorum</a></em> and the 8th-century <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-alamannorum">Lex Alamannorum</a></em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/konstanz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konstanz.jpg" alt="Konstanz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rheintorturm_in_Konstanz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JoachimKohler-HB, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alemanni changed the map of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Their pressure ended Roman rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agri-decumates">Agri Decumates</a> and shaped the late Roman frontier on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, with its forts, river patrols and fortified towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Constance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiseraugst">Kaiseraugst</a>. They were also part of Roman military life: according to a late Roman source, the Alemannic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crocus-alemannic-king">Crocus</a> supported the proclamation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> at York in 306, and several Alemannic nobles rose high in imperial service. After the Frankish conquest the Alemanni kept a distinct identity in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-alamannia">Duchy of Alamannia</a>, ruled by dukes under Frankish overlordship, until the Carolingian mayor of the palace Carloman suppressed its leaders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blood-court-at-cannstatt">Cannstatt</a> in 746. The region later became the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, one of the great duchies of the medieval German kingdom. The name lives on in many languages: French <em>Allemagne</em>, Spanish <em>Alemania</em>, Portuguese <em>Alemanha</em>, Turkish <em>Almanya</em> and Arabic <em>Almānyā</em> all name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after this one people. Their speech is the ancestor of the Alemannic dialects of German, including Swabian, Alsatian and Swiss German, still spoken across south-western Germany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, Liechtenstein and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The row-grave cemeteries of the region are one of the richest archaeological sources for early medieval Europe.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolbiac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolbiac.jpg" alt="Battle of Tolbiac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolbiac">Battle of Tolbiac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ary_Scheffer_-_Bataille_de_Tolbiac_496.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ary Scheffer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end of Alemannic independence came through the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. Around 496, traditionally at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tolbiac">Tolbiac</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulpich">Zülpich</a>) near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, the Alemanni fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> tells how Clovis, close to defeat, vowed to become a Christian if he won, and how the Alemanni submitted once their king had been killed. The date and place of this battle are uncertain, and many historians place the decisive defeat around 506, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a>, the Ostrogothic king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, wrote to Clovis asking him to spare the Alemanni who had fled into Ostrogothic territory. Some Alemannic groups then lived under Ostrogothic protection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>, roughly modern eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the Alpine foreland, while the northern lands fell to the Franks and were partly settled by Frankish newcomers. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, ceded their claims north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> to the Merovingian kings around 536–537, all the Alemanni came under Frankish rule. They were organised as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-alamannia">Duchy of Alamannia</a>, and Alemannic leaders fought in Frankish campaigns, including the invasion of Italy in 553–554 under the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuthari">Leuthari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butilin">Butilin</a>, whose armies were destroyed by disease and by the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, monasteries and Frankish law gradually transformed Alemannic society over the following two centuries.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Alemanni were the confederation that broke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontier in the 3rd century, fought emperors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> to Majorian, and settled the lands of later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, where their name survives in the Alemannic dialects and in the word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in many languages.</em></p>

